%N A174795 Number of admissible sequences of order j; related to 5x+1 problem.
%C A174795 From _Mike Winkler_, Sep 15 2026: Put alpha = log_2(5), m_n = floor(n*alpha), and delta_n = n*alpha - m_n. Then (1/n)*binomial(m_n-1,n-1) <= a(n) <= (1/n)*binomial(m_n,n-1). For n >= 2, equality in the lower bound holds exactly when delta_n is smaller than delta_j for every 1 <= j < n, and equality in the upper bound holds exactly when delta_n is larger than delta_j for every 1 <= j < n. Equivalently, the lower and upper equality orders are the denominators of the corresponding one-sided convergents and semiconvergents to log_2(5). At a lower equality order the value is binomial(m_n,n)/m_n, and at an upper equality order the value is binomial(m_n+1,n)/(m_n+1). Thus the extremal values are rational Catalan numbers. The first lower equality orders are 1, 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25, 28, 87, 146, 789, ... . The first upper equality orders are 1, 2, 3, 31, 59, 205, 351, 497, 643, ... . In particular a(2)=2, a(3)=5 and a(4)=14 agree with A000108(2), A000108(3) and A000108(4), respectively; the coincidence breaks at n=5, where a(5)=56 whereas A000108(5)=42.
%H A174795 Mike Winkler, <a href="https://doi.org/10.13140/RG.2.2.35856.78086">Admissible qx+1 Sequences, Semiconvergents, and Rational Catalan Numbers</a>, ResearchGate preprint, September 2026.
%F A174795 a(1)=1 and a(k+1) = Sum_{m=1..k}(-1)^(m-1)*binomial(floor((k-m+1)*(log(5)/log(2)))+m-1, m)*a(k-m+1) for k >= 1. - _Vladimir M. Zarubin_, Sep 25 2015
%F A174795 A finite sequence s(k), k=1,...,N, is admissible of order j if s(k)=5/2 exactly j times, s(k)=1/2 exactly N-j times, s(1)*...*s(N) < 1, and s(1)*...*s(m) > 1 for all 1 <= m < N. Let alpha = log_2(5). Then a(n) = #{(s_1,...,s_{n-1}) : 1 <= s_1 < ... < s_{n-1}, s_i <= floor(i*alpha) for 1 <= i < n}. - _Mike Winkler_, Sep 15 2026
%e A174795 The unique admissible sequence of order 1 is 5/2, 1/2, 1/2.
%e A174795 The two admissible sequences of order 2 are 5/2, 5/2, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2 and 5/2, 1/2, 5/2, 1/2, 1/2.
%t A174795 h[n_] := Module[{L = {{1}}}, For[i = 1, i <= n, i++, K = {}; S = 0; j = 1; While[5^i >= 2^(i + j - 1), If[5^(i - 1) >= 2^(i + j - 2), S = S + L[[i, j]]]; AppendTo[K, S]; j = j + 1]; AppendTo[L, K]; ]; Return[Map[Last, Drop[L, 1]]]]
%o A174795 (PARI)
%o A174795 n=20; a=vector(n); log52=log(5)/log(2);
%o A174795 {a[1]=1; for ( k=1, n-1, a[k+1]=sum( m=1,k,(-1)^(m-1)*binomial( floor( (k-m+1)*log52)+m-1,m)*a[k-m+1] );print1(a[k], ", ") );} \\ _Vladimir M. Zarubin_, Sep 25 2015
%Y A174795 Similar to A100982, but for the 5x+1 problem.
%Y A174795 Cf. A100982 and A174796, the analogous admissible-sequence counts for the 3x+1 and 7x+1 problems; A028508, the continued fraction expansion of log_2(5); A046104, the denominators of its convergents; A000108, whose terms agree with a(2), a(3), a(4).
